### Subject of the issue/enhancement/features
With the accordion, VoiceOver does not announce "expanded"/"collapsed" as the aria-expanded attribute is toggled (accordion opened/closed). (NVDA appears to announce it correctly).

This is violation of […] : "Name, Role, Value", so anybody using the accordion component will be non-compliant with […]

### Your environment
Bug may be reproduced on this adapt demo page
https://adaptlearning.github.io/v4demo/#/id/co-05
VoiceOver on Mac OS 10.15.x

### Steps to reproduce
Start up VoiceOver
Load the URL to the demo page into Safari.
With Keyboard, tab down to focus the first accordion button (labeled "Lists")
Press ENTER to expand it, and again to contract it.

### Expected behaviour
VoiceOver should announce "collapsed" or "expanded" when the accordion is toggled open and closed.

### Actual behaviour
VoiceOver does not announce the change, even though the aria-expanded attribute appears to get toggled correctly. If you tab off the button, then shift-tab back, VoiceOver announces the button's expanded state correctly. It's the toggling itself which does not get announced.

### Further Information:
Earlier versions of VoiceOver ignored aria-expanded, but that has been addressed in more recent versions. This is **not** a VoiceOver bug. [w3c has some disclosure examples](https://w3c.github.io/aria-practices/examples/disclosure/disclosure-navigation.html) which have the correct behavior with VoiceOver. 

Even stranger, the bug disappears if I remove all the adapt javaScript and CSS, and handle the accordion toggle with my own test script.

Here's how I tested that: I copied **all** the markup from inside the body tag of that demo page, and pasted it (unchanged) into the body of a new/empty HTML document. I then added the following bare-bones accordion-toggle script.
```
		<script>
var setupAcc = function (opener, opened) {
	var b = document.querySelector(opener);
	var m = document.querySelector(opened);
	var expand = function () {
			b.setAttribute("aria-expanded", "true");
			m.style.display="block";
	};
	var contract = function () {
			b.setAttribute("aria-expanded", "false");
			m.style.display="none";
	};
	var toggle = function () {
		var expanded = b.getAttribute("aria-expanded") === "true";
		if (expanded) {
			contract();
		} else {
			expand();
		}
	};
	b.onclick = toggle;
}
setupAcc(".accordion-item-title", ".accordion-item-body");
		</script>
```
Once again, tab down to the button labeled "Lists" and press ENTER
With this script, VoiceOver announces "expanded"/"collapsed" correctly. 

Note: The barebones script only hooks up the first of the three accordions, because it uses a class-based lookup of the button and accordion body.

The markup is identical in both cases, but of course there is no CSS or any other scripts from the 'real' adapt page. Very unlikely that CSS is the culprit, so I assume that the adapt javaScript is interfering with VoiceOver somehow. Unfortunately, I have no further clues. 

Good hunting!

