## Prerequisites


- [X] I have searched for similar features requests in both open and closed tickets and cannot find a duplicate.
- [X] The feature is still missing in the latest stable version of Elementor ( Elementor Pro. )


**What problem is your feature request going to solve? Please describe.**
Let me start by saying I am really impressed with Elementor's accessibility right out of the box. I work in accessibility testing for a living and it's fantastic. The problem this feature request aims to solve is adding an option that would ensure compliance with […]. In short, […] requires, "A mechanism is available to bypass blocks of content that are repeated on multiple Web pages". 

**Describe the solution you'd like**
The solution here would be pretty simple. Understanding that not everyone wants to use this feature, you could make it something that can be enabled via the plugin itself (or somewhere else...). All that needs to be done is to add the following HTML immediately following the <body> tag on each page:

`<a class="skip-main" href="#main">Skip to main content</a>`

From there, you would add the following styling to the elementor .css files, or to the <head> on the page:

```
a.skip-main {
    left:-999px;
    position:absolute;
    top:auto;
    width:1px;
    height:1px;
    overflow:hidden;
    z-index:-999;
}
a.skip-main:focus, a.skip-main:active {
    color: #fff;
    background-color:#000;
    left: auto;
    top: auto;
    width: 30%;
    height: auto;
    overflow:auto;
    margin: 10px 35%;
    padding:5px;
    border-radius: 15px;
    border:4px solid yellow;
    text-align:center;
    font-size:1.2em;
    z-index:999;
}
```

**Describe alternatives you've considered**
This can currently be done manually via plugins like "Header Footer Code Manager", but with all of the great accessibility features already built into Elementor, this seems like it would be a nice addition to have natively. It's also a pretty straight forward solution. 

**Additional context**
None - keep up the good work!

[[…] Target [link]) reads: 

> The size of the target for pointer inputs is at least 44 by 44 CSS pixels except when:
>- **Equivalent** - The target is available through an equivalent link or control on the same page that is at least 44 by 44 CSS pixels;
>- **Inline** - The target is in a sentence or block of text;
>- **User Agent Control** - The size of the target is determined by the user agent and is not modified by the author;
>- **Essential** - A particular presentation of the target is essential to the information being conveyed. 

This is a Level AAA success criterion, but worth considering, particularly on mobile devices. Currently Able Player buttons have min-width set to 24px, so 44px would be considerably larger.
